00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:32.500
and then the red is A

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:36.430
and the yellow is T and
this is like super accurate.

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:40.310
They physically pair up like
this and they're quite tight.

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:41.810
Pretend I didn't just do that.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:45.033
They're quite tight, they hold together.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:51.340
So the first part of a PCR is
we got to break them apart.

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:56.900
I can't copy this if I can't
read what these matches are.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:00.060
And you can imagine you're an enzyme,

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:03.740
if you just go like this, like
if you just touch the top,

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:05.800
you don't know what these are.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.810
But if you break them apart you can say,

00:28:08.810 --> 00:28:10.920
ah, I know what this piece is.

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:15.343
I have three bumps and it's
short, so I know it's a C.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:21.093
This one has three bumps, but
it's long so it must be a G.

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:26.480
So the way that we break apart DNA

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:27.943
is called denaturing.

00:28:29.063 --> 00:28:30.850
You get it really, really hot

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:33.430
and then all of the pieces come apart.

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:35.620
And it looks just like that

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:38.170
where it separates into the two strands.

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:40.590
And then, like I said, the enzyme,

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:44.270
sorry, balancing a little, can
go in and read these and say,

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:48.440
okay, short three, that must be a C,

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:52.210
long, two, that must be an A,

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:54.710
short, three, that's a C,

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:57.100
short, two, that's a T.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:58.390
And then it'll go all the way through

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:01.793
and that's how the polymerase
reads what the letters are.

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:05.780
So what we have is a primer,

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:08.780
because if you open all of this up,

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:10.330
