00:48:33.572 --> 00:48:37.100
So you can imagine that when
you're like swabbing someone's

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:40.560
soda can you're not
getting that much DNA off,

00:48:40.560 --> 00:48:43.500
you getting like individual pieces.

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:47.830
And by using PCR, you can
make more and more of it.

00:48:47.830 --> 00:48:50.510
And by making more than
you can go in and say,

00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:53.513
okay, what is each letter of that thing?

00:48:56.200 --> 00:48:59.890
And by knowing each letter,
then you can start to know,

00:48:59.890 --> 00:49:01.313
okay, who is this?

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:03.763
Similarly who is this?

00:49:05.380 --> 00:49:08.800
This is one of the ways you
can do paternity testing.

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:13.637
So if you amplify a gene,
maybe let's say for,

00:49:15.858 --> 00:49:17.980
I don't know, a hair color

00:49:19.030 --> 00:49:22.860
and you amplify it off of the

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:26.140
parent one and parent two.

00:49:26.140 --> 00:49:31.090
So the maternal and paternal
parents and off of the child,

00:49:31.090 --> 00:49:33.820
you can say, okay, well,

00:49:33.820 --> 00:49:36.080
does this gene match

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:37.300
what

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.550
the two parents have given them?

00:49:39.550 --> 00:49:40.600
Because you know that

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:46.400
humans are diploid.

00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:50.330
We have one copy of DNA
from the maternal side

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:53.770
and one copy of DNA from the paternal side

00:49:53.770 --> 00:49:56.867
and that's how you get
two copies at the end.

00:49:56.867 --> 00:50:01.640
Di-ploid two - I don't know
what ploid means but two of them

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:03.623
I guess, chromosomes, your genomes.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:06.923
So by knowing,

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:11.100
oh, sorry, I realized
I have a hanging nail.

00:50:13.210 --> 00:50:14.043
By

00:50:15.250 --> 00:50:16.690
checking that these match

00:50:16.690 --> 00:50:20.760
the maternal and paternal
genomes, you can find out,

00:50:20.760 --> 00:50:24.740
oh, if this doesn't match
you're probably not the parent

00:50:24.740 --> 00:50:27.593
or if this does match,
yeah, you are the parent.

